          <h2><span class="i18n-en">Wet-Lab Contributions: Reusable Parts for <span class="keep-together">Yeast Engineering</span></span></h2>
          <div class="content-body">
            <p><span class="i18n-en">Our wet-lab contribution is a set of documented genetic components and characterization results that future teams can use when engineering yeast signaling systems. The parts cover pheromone-responsive reporting, <i>FAR1</i>/<i>SST2</i> editing and <i>GPA1</i> C-terminal modification. Their sequences and intended roles are indexed on our Parts page, while construction and experimental evidence are documented on our Engineering and Results pages. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            

            
              
            
            <h3 id="wetlab-reporter-parts" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">1. Pheromone-responsive reporter parts</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Featured parts:</strong> BBa_26J53MF4 — <i>FUS1</i> promoter · BBa_26D8D1SG — <i>FUS1</i>–<i>yEGFP</i> reporter fragment</span></p>

            <figure class="contrib-figure">
              <img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6373/wiki/lab/project/fig1-pesc-his-fus1-yegfp-map.avif" alt="Annotated plasmid map of pESC-HIS-P_FUS1-yEGFP with the FUS1 promoter, the yEGFP reporter gene, the CYC1 terminator and the vector backbone elements labelled." width="1188" height="1150" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>
                <span class="contrib-figure__caption">Figure 1. Plasmid map of pESC-HIS-P_FUS1-yEGFP.</span>
                <span class="contrib-figure__desc">The 7,181-bp plasmid contains the <i>FUS1</i> promoter, the <i>yEGFP</i> reporter gene, and a downstream <i>CYC1</i> terminator, together with the annotated backbone elements. The map shows the plasmid context of the <i>FUS1</i> promoter and <i>FUS1</i>–<i>yEGFP</i> reporter fragment contributed by our team, rather than the boundaries of either individual Registry part. Map generated using SnapGene.</span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">We provide a defined 435-bp <i>FUS1</i> promoter fragment and a 1,152-bp composite fragment combining this promoter with the <i>yEGFP</i> coding sequence. The promoter is recorded separately from the reporter, allowing future teams to reuse the regulatory element or the assembled fluorescence readout according to their own designs. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">We characterized the reporter in BY4741 using α-factor induction, fluorescence imaging, time-course measurements and growth assays. These experiments provide a reference for evaluating downstream yeast pheromone-pathway output and comparing engineered chassis strains. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Value for future teams:</strong> a characterized reporter starting point for yeast signaling studies. The composite is a reporter fragment, not a complete standalone expression system; a suitable vector and transcriptional termination context must be supplied. Its α-factor characterization does not establish HA detection. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/design.html">Design</a></span></span></p>

            <h3 id="wetlab-far1-sst2" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">2. <i>FAR1</i>/<i>SST2</i> editing components</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Featured composite parts:</strong> BBa_26IKMSKO — <i>FAR1</i> sgRNA expression cassette · BBa_26YVX4QT — <i>SST2</i> sgRNA expression cassette</span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Matching repair templates:</strong> BBa_26HN2W3G — <i>FAR1</i> repair template · BBa_262SR0ED — <i>SST2</i> repair template</span></p>

            <figure class="contrib-figure">
              <img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6373/wiki/lab/project/fig2-pml104-vector-map.avif" alt="Annotated map of the pML104 vector used for yeast editing, showing URA3, the SNR52 promoter, the SUP4 terminator and other labelled vector features." width="1058" height="1142" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>
                <span class="contrib-figure__caption">Figure 2. Map of the pML104 vector used in the yeast-editing workflow.</span>
                <span class="contrib-figure__desc">The 11,258-bp map displays annotated vector features, including <i>URA3</i>, the <i>SNR52</i> promoter, and the <i>SUP4</i> terminator. The <i>FAR1</i>- and <i>SST2</i>-targeting sequences are not explicitly identified in this view; therefore, this figure presents the vector context rather than a target-specific sgRNA part map. Map generated using SnapGene.</span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">We provide target-specific sgRNA expression cassettes and matching repair templates for <i>FAR1</i> and <i>SST2</i> editing in BY4741. Each cassette combines a yeast RNA polymerase III promoter, a target-specific spacer, the SpCas9 sgRNA scaffold and a terminator. The repair templates are designed to introduce frameshift-producing four-base-pair deletions. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">Following an unsuccessful initial construction round, we revised guide assembly and repair-template design. Colony screening and Sanger sequencing subsequently identified the intended <i>FAR1</i> and <i>SST2</i> edits, and growth and fluorescence measurements characterized the resulting chassis under α-factor induction. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Value for future teams:</strong> matched targeting and repair components, together with a documented editing workflow and genotype-to-phenotype evaluation. These components require Cas9 and the appropriate donor and host context; the sgRNA cassette alone is not a complete knockout system. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a></span></span></p>

            <h3 id="wetlab-gpa1-module" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">3. A Gpa1 C-terminal engineering module</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Featured basic part:</strong> BBa_26EG2LOR — Gαq-derived EYNLV sequence with stop codon</span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Associated composite:</strong> BBa_266K1VDV — <i>GPA1</i> C-terminal humanization cassette</span></p>

            <figure class="contrib-figure">
              <img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6373/wiki/lab/project/fig4-gpa1-humanization-map.avif" alt="Partial annotated map of the GPA1 C-terminal humanization construct, showing the humanization sequence and the adjacent URA3-associated annotations." width="1704" height="296" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>
                <span class="contrib-figure__caption">Figure 4. Partial annotated map of the <i>GPA1</i> C-terminal humanization construct.</span>
                <span class="contrib-figure__desc">The view shows the region labeled as the humanization sequence and the adjacent <i>URA3</i>-associated annotations. It provides local construct context but does not delineate the complete donor boundaries or both homology arms. Map generated using SnapGene.</span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">BBa_26EG2LOR is an 18-bp module encoding the Gαq-derived C-terminal sequence EYNLV followed by a stop codon. We used this sequence in a <i>GPA1</i>-editing design intended to replace the native Gpa1 C-terminal pentapeptide KIGII. It is a replacement segment, not a complete G-protein coding sequence. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">After revising the donor-construction strategy to overlap-extension fusion PCR, we obtained strains 12 and 17 with the expected target-region sequence. The humanized chassis was further characterized through α-factor-induced morphology, growth and <i>FUS1</i>–<i>yEGFP</i> fluorescence measurements, which showed attenuated native pheromone responses. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Value for future teams:</strong> a defined replacement module and a documented starting point for studying yeast G-protein interfaces. The evidence supports the edited-chassis phenotype; it does not independently establish hM1Dq coupling or attribute every observed change solely to the 18-bp replacement. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            <h3 id="wetlab-records" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">Reusable construction and troubleshooting records</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">Alongside these parts, we document unsuccessful construction attempts and the design changes that followed, including revised sgRNA assembly, repair-template redesign and the switch from a multi-round donor-construction route to overlap-extension fusion PCR. These records give future teams practical alternatives to evaluate rather than only presenting the final successful constructs. <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a></span></span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><strong>Explore the resources:</strong> <span class="contrib-refs"><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/parts.html">Parts</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}project/engineering.html">Engineering</a> · <a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}wet-lab/result.html">Results</a></span></span></p>

            <h3 id="wetlab-validation" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">From Successful Engineering to Functional Validation</span></h3>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">Our experimental work addresses two specific questions: how can the native signaling regulation of yeast be reliably modified, and how can we determine whether precise engineering of the G-protein interface changes cellular signaling behavior?</span></p>

              
            
              
            <h4 id="wetlab-genediting" class="content-subh4"><span class="i18n-en">Translating Gene Editing into Verifiable Phenotypes</span></h4>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">Obtaining the target strain was not the endpoint of our work. During the editing of <i>FAR1</i> and <i>SST2</i>, our initial construction attempt failed to yield the desired strains, prompting us to revise our construction strategy. We ultimately confirmed the intended genetic modifications by sequencing. The resulting strains carried a 4-bp deletion in each target gene, providing clear sequence-level evidence for subsequent validation.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">More importantly, we combined genotype verification with growth and fluorescence characterization under α-factor induction. Comparing the wild-type, single-knockout, and double-knockout strains allowed us to determine whether the effects of gene deletion extended beyond the DNA level to cellular growth and reporter output. Our results showed that the double knockout altered the growth response to stimulation and produced higher reporter fluorescence under the tested conditions. These findings support the functional characterization of the engineered chassis beyond successful strain construction alone.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <h4 id="wetlab-construction" class="content-subh4"><span class="i18n-en">Refining Construction Methods to Ensure Sequence Accuracy</span></h4>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">Unlike the double-knockout strategy, <i>GPA1</i> engineering required precise replacement of five amino acids at the C-terminus of the protein. Our initial strategy, which combined Gibson Assembly with multiple rounds of PCR, resulted in sequence errors in the donor fragment. We subsequently adopted overlap-extension fusion PCR to reorganize the homology arms and selection cassette. This revised strategy yielded strains 12 and 17, whose target sequences were confirmed by sequencing. The modification replaced the native C-terminal pentapeptide KIGII with EYNLV, corresponding to the human Gαq C-terminal sequence.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">The value of this work lies not only in obtaining the correctly modified strains, but also in documenting why the construction strategy was changed, how the alternative approach addressed the initial problem, and how the final sequence was verified. These details provide a practical reference for researchers undertaking precise genomic modifications in yeast, offering more transferable information than a positive PCR result alone.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <h4 id="wetlab-connecting" class="content-subh4"><span class="i18n-en">Connecting Sequence Modifications to Cellular Behavior</span></h4>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">Both engineering strategies required us to address a question beyond successful construction: did the modified cells exhibit the expected functional changes? For the double-knockout strategy, growth curves and fluorescence output were used to examine the relationship between signaling responses and cellular state. For <i>GPA1</i> humanization, we further investigated growth and morphological changes under α-factor induction.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">The current results indicate that <i>GPA1</i>-humanized yeast exhibits altered phenotypes associated with native pheromone stimulation. However, these changes do not by themselves demonstrate that a heterologous GPCR has successfully coupled to the downstream yeast signaling pathway. In addition, the descriptions of fluorescence responses in different sections of our Wiki are inconsistent. The direction and magnitude of this response must therefore be determined by re-examining the original data before a definitive conclusion can be drawn. Distinguishing confirmed findings from observations that remain unresolved is an important part of the experimental knowledge we make available to future researchers.</span></p>

              
            
              
            
            <h3 id="wetlab-resources" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">Reusable Design Resources for Yeast Signaling Chassis Engineering</span></h3>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">We organized two core aspects of our yeast chassis engineering—native signaling regulation and G-protein input-interface reprogramming—into biological parts that can be independently accessed, adapted, combined, and further validated. These resources provide concrete sequence references and starting points for future yeast signaling engineering projects.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <h4 id="wetlab-strategy" class="content-subh4"><span class="i18n-en">A Directly Accessible Genetic Engineering Strategy for Yeast</span></h4>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">We submitted the <i>FAR1</i> and <i>SST2</i> sgRNA expression cassettes (BBa_26IKMSKO and BBa_26YVX4QT, respectively), together with their corresponding repair templates (BBa_26HN2W3G and BBa_262SR0ED), to the iGEM Registry. Each repair template incorporates a 4-bp deletion and a PAM-site modification designed to disrupt the target gene and reduce the likelihood of repeated recognition by the same sgRNA following editing.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">These parts provide sequence-level design references for researchers seeking to modify cell-cycle responses or negative regulation of G-protein signaling in yeast. By consulting the published sequences and construct information, users can assess their suitability after checking the target locus, PAM sequence, and homology regions in their own strain, rather than designing the entire editing system from scratch.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <h4 id="wetlab-starting" class="content-subh4"><span class="i18n-en">A Sequence-Level Starting Point for Cross-Species Receptor Coupling</span></h4>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">We submitted the <i>GPA1</i> C-terminal humanization cassette with a <i>URA3</i> selection marker (BBa_266K1VDV), as well as a basic part encoding the Gαq-derived pentapeptide EYNLV followed by a stop codon (BBa_26EG2LOR). The composite cassette contains upstream and downstream homology arms, the target replacement sequence, and the <i>URA3</i> selection module, providing a defined construct for targeted modification of the yeast <i>GPA1</i> locus.</span></p>

              
            
              
            <p><span class="i18n-en">The target sequences in strains 12 and 17 were verified, providing experimental evidence that the intended sequence replacement was achieved. For teams seeking to connect heterologous GPCRs to yeast signaling networks, this construct offers a concrete starting point for further design and comparison. Nevertheless, successful sequence replacement does not establish effective coupling of the human receptor to the yeast signaling pathway; downstream signal transmission must be validated independently.</span></p>

              
            
              
