        <div id="contribution-hp" class="content-card">

          <h2><span class="i18n-en">Human Practices Contributions</span></h2>
          <div class="content-body">

            <p><span class="i18n-en"><em>Preface to Influenza A in the Air.</em> After our social practice, we recognised that many important questions about influenza A virus in the air — how it is transmitted, how long it can persist in air and on surfaces, how environmental conditions affect it, and what different detection signals can actually establish — draw on several fields and are dispersed across the literature. We therefore read the relevant studies and organised what we learned into this <i>Influenza A in the Air</i> contribution, bringing together the evidence, key data, study conditions and references needed to examine these questions. The document does not present new experimental findings; it structures existing research into a continuous, accessible and traceable resource that can help readers develop an overall understanding of airborne transmission, environmental persistence, detection and control of influenza A. In this way, it provides a reference for learning, discussion and project development, reduces duplicated searching, and makes the material available for further use, checking and improvement within the iGEM community.</span></p>

