            <h3 id="hp-apic" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">The discussion format: APiC question cards</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">Our Human Practices work produced a second, less obvious contribution: a format for peer discussion. At the APiC project exchange we replaced the usual sequence of project presentations with structured question cards built around iGEM award tracks. Each card directed discussion through five dimensions — value positioning, design rationale, analysis of previous projects, critical self-evaluation, and future optimisation — so that teams spent their time examining why a design was appropriate rather than describing what it aimed to do.</span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">The format proved reusable because it does not depend on shared subject matter. Teams working on different biological questions can use the same cards, because the questions are about reasoning rather than about pathogens. The cards and the process are documented so that future organisers can adapt them to other award tracks, exchange events and review sessions.</span></p>

            <ul class="link-list">

              <li><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}human-practices/integrated human-practices.html#section-apic"><span class="i18n-en">Related resource: Human Practices — APiC Conference</span></a></li>

            </ul>

            <h3 id="hp-yeast-workshop" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">The workshop record: making experimental experience shareable</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">The third contribution came out of an online yeast workshop that brought together CAU-China, NEFU-China, SZPU-China, Keystone, NKU and SHSID. The discussion covered transformation efficiency, gene knockout, selection stability, reporter leakage, membrane-protein expression and localisation, product formats and biosafety. Much of what made it useful was not the methods themselves but the troubleshooting attached to them: which failure modes were recognised, which controls distinguished them, and what remained unresolved.</span></p>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">Two lessons from that discussion are recorded in the workshop write-up. The first is a methodological caution: a mutation at a target site does not by itself establish that Cas9 cleavage caused it, so controls and independent genotype verification are needed before a knockout is claimed. The second is that methods shared by other teams should be preserved with their attribution — CAU-China's combination of fluorescently tagged receptors, FM4-64 membrane staining and confocal microscopy, and the alginate microsphere and freeze-dried colour-card formats discussed by Keystone and NKU, are recorded as theirs. None of the methods discussed originated with us; the value of the record lies in the exchange itself and in keeping each method attached to the team that described it.</span></p>

            <ul class="link-list">

              <li><a href="{{ basePath | default('') }}human-practices/integrated human-practices.html#expert-yeast-online"><span class="i18n-en">Related resource: Human Practices — Online Yeast Workshop</span></a></li>

            </ul>

            <h3 id="hp-sharing-lessons" data-no-fold><span class="i18n-en">What we learned about sharing knowledge</span></h3>

            <p><span class="i18n-en">Three things became clear across these activities. Structured questions produce better exchanges than presentations, because they force specificity. Documented troubleshooting is more valuable than documented success, because most teams fail in similar ways. And a contribution is strongest when it preserves attribution, because that is what allows the next team to build on it rather than repeat it.</span></p>

